318	\toprule
319	Family & Pattern & Reasonable & Full & Nearest transition \\
320	\midrule
321	Qwen2.5-14B & Collapse & 100\% & 100\% & $\tau = 9.4$ \\
322	Gemma-2 9B & Benefit & 100\% & 89\% & $\tau = 3.8$ \\
323	Phi-4 14.7B & Immune & 100\% & 82\% & $\tau < 0.35$ \\
324	OLMo-2 7B & Benefit & 100\% & 82\% & $\tau = 3.25$ \\
325	Qwen2.5-7B & V-recovery & 100\% & 72\% & $\tau = 3.0$ \\
326	\bottomrule
327	\end{tabular}
328	\end{table}
329	
330	Within the reasonable parameter range, all five families achieve 100\% classification stability (150/150 family--parameter combinations). Transitions occur only at extreme $\tau$ values well beyond the default ($\tau\!=\!2$): Qwen-14B requires $\tau\!=\!9.4$ (4.7$\times$ default) to reclassify, reflecting the large separation between its $\Delta\text{acc}$ ($-$9.4pp) and any plausible noise threshold. Qwen-7B is the least robust at $\tau\!=\!3.0$ (1.5$\times$ default), but this is still above any defensible noise threshold for MMLU. The high intrinsic effect-size separation---Phi-4 fluctuates $<$0.4pp while Qwen-14B declines by 9.4pp---means that classification stability is driven by the data rather than by parameter choice.
331	
332	\section{Cross-Family Domain Decomposition}
333	\label{sec:domain_decomposition}
334	
335	We decompose MMLU accuracy changes into four constituent domains (STEM, Humanities, Social Sciences, Other) across all model families at each contamination dosage.
336	
337	Two patterns emerge. First, the direction of accuracy change is governed by the dose-response pattern, not by domain. At d100, Collapse (Qwen-14B) produces negative $\Delta\text{acc}$ in all four domains (STEM $-6.8$pp, Humanities $-10.1$pp, Social Sciences $-11.2$pp, Other $-9.4$pp), while Benefit (Gemma-2) produces positive $\Delta\text{acc}$ throughout ($+2.7$, $+3.9$, $+5.0$, $+3.4$pp). Immune (Phi-4) remains within $\pm 1.0$pp across all domains. No pattern produces a crossover exceeding the noise band ($|\Delta| > 1$pp); the single exception is V-recovery Humanities ($+0.28$pp, well within sampling noise). This consistency holds at intermediate dosages as well.
338	
339	Second, domains differ in sensitivity. STEM is the most resilient (mean $|\Delta\text{acc}| = 2.8$pp across all families at d100). Social Sciences is the most sensitive at $5.1$pp---$1.8\times$ the STEM magnitude. The ordering is stable across patterns: in Collapse, STEM shows the smallest decline; in Benefit, STEM shows the smallest gain.
340	
341	These results reinforce the global capability modulation interpretation (\S\ref{sec:memorization_test}): contamination direction is determined by the model's dose-response pattern rather than domain-specific memorization. The differential domain sensitivity more likely reflects variation in item difficulty distributions than differential memorization rates.
342	
